.TH HD-SECBOOT 8 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-secboot \- control Secure Boot with the hyprdebian Secure Boot keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-secboot \-\-status
.RB [ \-q ]
.br
.B hd-secboot
.BR \-\-generate " | " \-\-store " | " \-\-clear " | " \-\-enable " | " \-\-disable
.br
.B hd-secboot \-\-append\-rom\-hash
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-secboot
generates the Secure Boot keys of the owner in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/secboot ,
writes them in the firmware and removes them again, turns Secure Boot on
and off, and adds the hashes of option ROMs to db.
See
.BR hd-secboot (7)
for the keys.
It does one of these at a time.
.PP
.B \-\-status
does not need root; the others run as root.
.PP
The keys go in in this order: the firmware is put in setup mode, without a
platform key, then
.B \-\-store
writes db and KEK, and
.B \-\-enable
writes PK, which ends setup mode.
.B \-\-disable
removes PK again, and
.B \-\-clear
empties db and KEK, which needs setup mode as well.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-status
Say whether Secure Boot is on, and why not when it is off: it is turned off
in the setup menu of the firmware, the firmware is in setup mode, the
firmware does not support Secure Boot, or the system was not booted with
UEFI.
Then list what PK, KEK and db hold: the subject of each certificate and each
SHA-256 hash.
It reads the EFI variables in
.IR /sys/firmware/efi/efivars ,
which every user can read.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
With
.BR \-\-status :
say nothing, only set the exit status.
.TP
.B \-\-generate
Generate the keys in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/secboot ,
unless that directory exists already: keys that may be in the firmware are
never replaced.
The postinst of
.B hd-base
runs this, so the installer has keys to sign the bootloader with.
The keys are made in
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot.tmp
and moved into place when they are all there, so a failure never leaves half
a set behind.
.TP
.B \-\-store
Write db and KEK with the keys, from their signed updates
.I db.auth
and
.IR KEK.auth ,
with
.BR efi-updatevar (1).
They replace what db and KEK held, such as the certificates of Microsoft and
the hashes of option ROMs.
The firmware must be in setup mode.
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Empty db and KEK, with updates signed by KEK and PK.
The firmware must be in setup mode.
.TP
.B \-\-enable
Write PK from
.IR PK.auth ,
which ends setup mode, so Secure Boot is on from the next boot unless it is
turned off in the setup menu of the firmware.
The firmware must be in setup mode, and db and KEK must hold the keys, so
the firmware does not end up refusing everything that is signed with them.
Some firmware only leaves setup mode at the next boot; then it says so.
.TP
.B \-\-disable
Remove PK, with an update signed by the PK key, which puts the firmware in
setup mode: from the next boot, it checks no signatures and Secure Boot is
off.
db and KEK keep what they hold, so
.B \-\-enable
turns Secure Boot on again.
The PK in the firmware must be the one in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/secboot .
In setup mode, anyone with root can change db, KEK and PK without a
signature.
.TP
.B \-\-append\-rom\-hash
Add the SHA-256 hashes of option ROMs to db.
Some devices have a driver in their option ROM that the firmware has to
load before the system boots, such as the driver (GOP) that a graphics card
needs to show the setup menu of the firmware and the bootloader.
The firmware measures each driver it loads in the TPM event log
.IR /sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements ,
so while Secure Boot is off, the log has the hash of every option ROM, also
of those it would refuse with Secure Boot on.
.IP
It lists the hashes with the devices that hold them: the PCI address, the
vendor and device IDs, their names from
.I pci.ids
when that is installed, and the driver of the kernel.
A hash that db holds already is marked.
Then it asks which hashes to add: their numbers, separated by spaces or
commas,
.BR all ,
or nothing for none.
It adds the hashes that were picked to db, in an update signed by the KEK
key, and keeps what db held.
.IP
Secure Boot must be off: turned off with
.B \-\-disable
or in the setup menu of the firmware, followed by a reboot.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH NOTES
The updates that remove PK, db and KEK, and the one with the hashes, are
made in a temporary directory and removed again, so no file is left that
could do the same a second time.
.PP
Before each write, it clears the immutable flag that the kernel puts on the
variables in
.IR /sys/firmware/efi/efivars .
When a write fails, the firmware may not let the system write its keys;
enroll the
.I .auth
or
.I .cer
files from its setup menu instead.
.PP
The hash of an option ROM changes with an update of the firmware of the
device, such as the VBIOS of a graphics card.
Until the new hash is added, the firmware refuses the driver with Secure
Boot on; for a graphics card, the screen stays black until the kernel
starts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
With
.BR \-\-status :
0 when Secure Boot is on, 1 when it is not.
Otherwise 0 when it is done, 1 on an error.
2 on wrong arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot
The keys.
.TP
.I /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
The EFI variables, among them SecureBoot, SetupMode, PK, KEK and db.
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements
The TPM event log, with the hashes of the option ROMs.
.SH EXAMPLES
Turn Secure Boot on, with the firmware in setup mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-secboot \-\-store
hd-secboot \-\-enable
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add the hash of the driver of a graphics card to db:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-secboot \-\-disable
reboot
hd-secboot \-\-append\-rom\-hash
hd-secboot \-\-enable
reboot
.fi
.RE
.PP
In a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if ! hd-secboot \-\-status \-q; then
    echo "Secure Boot is off."
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-secboot (7),
.BR hd-report (8),
.BR efi-updatevar (1),
.BR sign-efi-sig-list (1),
.BR sbsign (1)
